          "text": "The user asks a conceptual question: some programs legitimately write to both stdout and stderr. Is the \"one channel empty\" rule right for her project, or should she support two-channel (both channels non-empty) cases?\n\nShe's asking for my opinion, briefly. I'm in plan mode, read-only.\n\nLet me think carefully and critically.\n\nThe observation is true: many real programs write simultaneously to stdout (results) and stderr (progress/diagnostics). E.g., a long-running tool that prints progress to stderr while emitting data to stdout. Unix convention: stdout data, stderr diagnostics — they can both be present at the same time.\n\nBut her programs (tasks 1–4) are short, non-interactive, non-logging. Per §4.8, \"Эхо пользовательского ввода — шум; в коротких интерактивных программах логировать почти нечего\". So on success: only stdout. On error: only stderr. There's no progress stream. So for her project, \"one channel empty\" is a correct *invariant of the tasks*, not a universal law.\n\nKey nuance: Is the rule \"the other channel is empty\" a property of the *model* (success=data only, error=diagnostic only) or a universal contract? It's a property of her program class. If a future task acquires progress logging (e.g., \"печать хода работы в stderr\" while data to stdout), then the rule would be wrong and she'd need two-channel support.\n\nAlso distinguish: the AGENTS §4.8 mentions logging (голое протоколирование) as layer 11 — but also says for short interactive programs there's almost nothing to log, and that's an honest conclusion. And the production logging block is after the evaluation period. So for now, no dual-channel.\n\nAlso note: the exit code doesn't tell which channel; the rule should be keyed to the *task's contract*. For success tasks, stderr empty; for error tasks stdout empty. Actually more precisely: in her tasks, on error the program emits diagnostics and no result; on success result and no diagnostics. So exactly one channel