          "text": "The user asks where to document the single-channel contract: \"известные ограничения\" or not a limitation.\n\nLet me think. It's not a limitation — it's an intended contract/invariant of the task class, not a temporary compromise or a known defect. The \"Известные ограничения проекта\" section is defined (README line 221-223): \"моменты, которые не доведены до желаемого вида... ограничения фазы; либо условное временное решение; либо особенность компонентов\". Single-channel is by design, not a deviation. So it belongs in the normal testing documentation — the `check` / tests section — as an invariant, not in limitations.\n\nBut there's a nuance: if the harness hard-codes \"other channel empty\" and that assumption could break if tasks evolve, then it's more of a \"current contract with a future caveat\". The caveat (when it becomes false) could be mentioned. But as a *limitation*? No — it's not a limitation, it's a designed property.\n\nI'd recommend: document positively in the testing section (README `/tests` bullet near check, or the \"тесты\" section) as \"контракт каналов: сверяемый канал с контентом, второй обязан быть пуст; E2E add_case так устроен\". And optionally note in \"Известные ограничения\" only if there's a real pending trigger — but I'd say no, keep limitations for actual compromises.\n\nHowever, consider: the hardcoded rule means a future dual-channel case can't be represented. Is that a \"limitation of the current phase\"? Arguably yes, similar to \"io-fail only on linux\" or \"contains unused\". Hmm. The section already contains forward-looking notes. But the user asked whether it's a limitation; my opinion: it's a contract, document as such; if she wants a forward note, put it as one line under limitations about \"harness models single-channel; dual-channel case would require extending add_case\" — but that risks treating a design decision as a defect.\n\nBest answer: not a limitation. Put it in the testing section describing