So the project already has a principled deferral on self-testing the test tooling, with an explicit trigger.\n\nSo my recommendation: don't write dedicated tests now. Do a one-time manual probe of the three branches (to confirm messages and no crash), record it in the journal as the check. The systematic self-test of the harness (probe exe) stays deferred under the existing trigger. Also note: the error branches are cosmetic/diagnostic; the suite's correctness doesn't depend on them.\n\nBut I should be critical of the user's reasoning too: \"это тестовая обвязка и потому тесты лишне\" — the argument isn't \"it's test tooling\" per se; test tooling that can silently pass tests incorrectly IS worth testing. The correct argument is: (a) the correctness-critical paths of check are already covered transitively by 500+ comparisons including negative control; (b) the uncovered branches are input-validation for human misuse with near-zero blast radius; (c) there's already a deferred trigger for a systematic self-test. So agree with the conclusion, but for sharper reasons.\n\nAlso, one nuance: if you want a cheap middle ground, `WILL_FAIL`-style ctest entries assert \"misuse → nonzero exit\" without a probe exe. But as noted, that doesn't verify the message and a crash would also satisfy it. Marginal value. Could mention as an option but lean against.\n\nLet me also consider: the user is at the end of an evaluation period; the mentor grades. Would a mentor want self-testing of the harness? The course emphasizes \"код, который никогда не исполнялся, — код на доверии\" (§4.9.7). But that's about the program under test and error branches. For the harness, the pragmatic norm is: at least one manual smoke of misuse. I'd recommend a manual probe + journal note.\n\nSo answer:\n- [МНЕНИЕ, ВЫСОКИЙ] Dedicated automated tests: no, not now — agree with your conclusion, but reason is blast radius + transitive coverage, not \"it's test tooling\".\n- Recommend one-time manual probe of the 3 branches + journal line.\n- Existing deferral trigger (journal 2026-09-21) still governs systematic self-test (probe exe) — cite it.\n- If ever a second consumer appears or these branches grow logic, revisit.\n\nAlso mention what a manual probe should check: `check` no args → argc<2 message, exit 1; `check --foo x y` → unknown flag, exit 1; `check --equal a` → arity message + argv list, exit 1; `check --empty a b` → arity, exit 1; and importantly none crash (the argv indices). Capture the outputs.\n\nI should also flag: the arity message change (i=1) and unknown-flag branches are exactly where we had bugs (argv, format fields) — so a manual pass has real value to confirm they don't crash and read sensibly.\n\nPlan mode: no edits. Just answer. Keep concise.\n\nLet me write.\n\nI might use the question tool? No, they asked for opinion; answer directly.\n\nLet me keep it tight.",
