oing --help, create a separate module for CLI help/version. That's an architectural consideration.\n\nSo the user wants to implement --help. Key questions:\n- Scope: all 8 tasks.\n- Content of help per task: usage line, description, arguments, exit codes? Keep minimal.\n- Where output: stdout, exit 0 (recommend). `-h` alias too.\n- Where does the code live: currently `handle_version_flag` in string_utils; the plan was to move version + add help into a dedicated module (e.g., `common/cli_help`). This is the architectural axis: structure of modules / where CLI concerns live.\n- Behavior interaction with existing: t4 requires arg; if `--help` is passed, handle before radius parsing. t3 reads stdin; `--help` should short-circuit before reading. t1/t2 similarly.\n- Tests: add `--help` acceptance tests (stdout content + exit 0 + stderr empty) via add_case; and the non-stream channel empty. Help text is stable → an expect file. Or generated? Help text likely differs per task; store expect files in tests/expect.\n- Version interplay: handle `--help` and `--version`; precedence if both? Convention: first recognized flag wins, or help takes precedence. Decide.\n- Args parsing: currently t4 has argc/argv parse; t1/t2/t3 got argc/argv added for version. Need to handle help for all.\n\nAlso should `-h` be supported? The course mentions `--help`/`-h`. Could include.\n\nAlso: \"При неверном вводе — короткое сообщение + указание на --help\". Currently error messages don't mention --help. Adding this touches every diagnostic message (t1-t4, C and C++), which is a broader change (§4.6 style + §2.1.A). Is that in scope? The user said layer 10 not done is `--help`. The \"указание на --help\" is part of the layer 10 description. I should ask whether to include the hint in errors or keep scope to just the flag.\n\nAlso: where does `--help` fit with the single-channel contract? Help goes to stdout on success (exit 0), so stdout is the content channel, stderr empty → consistent with our contract. If help went to stderr, then... exit 0 but stderr non-empty would violate the contract as modeled. So help→stdout is also consistent with the new contract. Good argument for stdout.\n\nLet me now explore the code to give a precise plan:\n- common/string_utils.h/.hpp and .c/.cpp: handle_version_flag.\n- each main.c/.cpp: main structure and argc/argv.\n- tests/cases.cmake: version tests.\n- README versioning section.\n\nLet me read the relevant files. Let me look at common/string_utils.hpp/.h and one main (t4_cpp, t1_c, t3_c) and version.h.in.\n\nLet me explore.\n\nSince I'm in plan mode, I can read files. Let me do a batch.\n\nLet me read:\n- common/string_utils.hpp\n- common/string_utils.h\n- common/string_utils.cpp (handle_version_flag impl)\n- t4_filter_cpp/main.cpp\n- t4_filter_c/main.c\n- t3_bbox_cpp/main.cpp\n- t1_dist_matrix_c/main.c\n- t2_passport_cpp/main.cpp\n\nThat's a lot. Let me start with string_utils and t4 (most complex arg handling), then t1/t2/t3.\n\nLet me also check the course material for the exact --help convention. Let me grep 02-praktika.md around line 284 and 291.\n\nLet me batch reads.\n\nActually, let me first grep for \"help\" in the repo and in 02-praktika.md.",
