          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "I now have enough. Let me also check t4_c reads stdin and its radius check; yes.\n\nNow, one important nuance I noticed: `handle_version_flag` in string_utils.cpp uses `INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION` which comes from `version.h` generated. And `string_utils` is compiled into 3 library targets per language (static t1/t2, shared t3/t4). A new `cli.cpp` module would similarly need inclusion in all library target source lists (add to each add_library's sources) — or make it a separate library. The existing pattern: `string_utils.cpp` appears in common_cpp_static, string_utils_cpp_static, common_cpp_shared. If we create cli.cpp, we'd add it to all three per language, OR just keep it inside string_utils to avoid CMake churn.\n\nHmm, this is important for the plan: creating a new module means editing common/CMakeLists.txt in 3 targets × 2 languages = 6 places, plus new files. The journal's intent was a dedicated module. But there's also the t1/t2 static targets that only include geometry (t1) or string_utils (t2). Wait: `common_cpp_static` = geometry.cpp + string_utils.cpp (used by t1 and t2). `string_utils_cpp_static` = string_utils.cpp (used by t2?). Hmm, seems there's duplication/redundancy. Let me not overanalyze.\n\nIf we make a separate `cli` module, we need it in every target that links the mains. Simpler: add `cli.cpp`/`cli.c` to the same source lists as `string_utils` everywhere. That's mechanical.\n\nAlternatively, keep `handle_version_flag` and add `handle_help_flag` in string_utils (pragmatic), and note the module extraction as a follow-up. But the journal explicitly deferred the module to \"when doing --help\". So doing --help is the trigger to extract. I should present this as the architectural decision.\n\nActually, wait: is extracting a module worth it? The functions are tiny. The cost: new files, CMake edits in 6 places, include changes in 8 mains. The benefit: `--version`/`--help` are CLI concerns, not string utilities; conceptual cleanliness. The journal author herself wrote \"по-хорошему в отдельную программу потому что --version - не про работу со строками\". So she leans to extraction. I'll present both and recommend extraction (aligned with her stated intent), but note the CMake cost.\n\nHmm, but there's also the option of a single shared helper in common that takes the usage text, vs each program printing its own help. Let me think about the cleanest design:\n\nDesign A (handler takes usage text):\n```cpp\n// common/cli.hpp\nCOMMON_API bool handle_cli_flag(int argc, char* argv[], std::string_view usage, std::string_view version);\n// returns true if it handled --help/--version (printed and caller should return 0)\n```\nBut C variant needs `const char*`.\n\nActually version printing is identical across programs, so handler can print version itself (needs version.h include in cli.cpp). Help text differs → pass usage string.\n\n```cpp\n// C++: bool handle_help_flag(int argc, char* argv[], std::string_view usage);\n// C:   bool handle_help_flag(int argc, char* argv[], const char* usage);\n```\nEach main defines its usage text and calls. But that duplicates the usage text between C and C++ (they're separate files anyway, so duplication is inherent to dual implementation — acceptable, the project already duplicates diagnostics per side).\n\nDesign B (unified handler for both flags):\n```cpp\nenum class cli_flag { none, help, version };\n```\nOverkill.\n\nSimplest consistent with existing `handle_version_flag`: add `handle_help_flag(argc, argv, usage)` returning bool, printing usage to stdout and returning true. And keep `handle_version_flag` as-is. Call both at top:\n```cpp\nif (handle_version_flag(argc, argv)) return 0;\nif (handle_help_flag(argc, argv, usage_text)) return 0;\n```\nOr combine into `handle_cli_flags(argc, argv, usage)`. The journal wanted to consolidate version+help into a module. A single `handle_cli_flags` that checks version first, then help, is cleaner and avoids two calls. But then help needs usage text. So:\n```cpp\nCOMMON_API bool handle_cli_flags(int argc, char* argv[], const char* usage);\n```\nHmm but version doesn't need usage. Passing usage always is fine.\n\nBut `handle_version_flag` is already used and tested. Changing its name touches all 8. That's part of the refactor.\n\nI think the plan should present the decision clearly and let the user pick. Given the instruction \"ask when weighing tradeoffs\", I'll ask.\n\nNow for the question set:\n1. Scope of layer 10: only `--help` (+`-h`), or also add \"see --help\" hint to usage-class error messages?\n2. Where help output: stdout/exit 0 (recommended) vs stderr.\n3. Module structure: extract `common/cli` (move version + add help) vs keep in string_utils.\n4. Help text: per-task, C and C++ byte-identical (shared expect) vs independent.\n5. Precedence/arg handling: `--help`/`-h` only with argc==2 (like version), or help regardless of other args.\n\nThat's a lot of questions; I'll consolidate. But first, let me confirm what \"everything else from layer 10\" is present, so I answer part 2.\n\nLayer 10 items and status:\n- argc/argv — ✓\n- --help — ✗\n- --version — ✓\n- stdout/stderr separation — ✓\n- differentiated exit codes 64/65/66/74 — ✓\n- pipe-friendly — ✓\nPlus from §2.1.A row:\n- usage-строка — ✗ (no usage text yet; only terse error messages)\n- `-h` — ✗\n- \"указание на --help\" in errors — ✗\n- \"понимает, почему usage в stderr\" — conceptual, satisfied by current design.\n\nAlso §5 layer 10 mentions exactly those. So the only missing pieces are: `--help` (+ usage text + `-h`), and optionally the \"see --help\" hint.\n\nNow, another thing: the version tests exist (`-R \"_version\"`), and add_case supports args. So help tests fit easily.\n\nNow, about expect files: help text is static per task. Following §4.9.3 \"эталоны генерятся на лету, в репо не хранятся\"?? Wait — but version test generates expect on the fly. For help, the help text is *authored content*, not derivable from a reference computation. So it must be stored (like the other t1-test1 etc. expect files). Actually §4.9.3 says expect files for program outputs are generated on the fly by an independent reference — but for help text, there's no independent reference; the help text IS the specification. So storing it in tests/expect is the only option. That's consistent with existing tests/expect (t1_test1 etc. are stored). Actually wait, the project does store expect files in tests/expect. So storing t*_help is fine, despite §4.9.3 (which is about algorithmic outputs; help is an authored string). I'll note this.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: help text is the same as what the program prints; storing it means the test compares program output to a hand-written copy. That's normal for help/manpage tests. Acceptable. Could also assert key substrings with `--contains`, but we moved to `--equal`. Use `--equal` with a stored expect. Fine.\n\nNow the C/C++ parity: if C and C++ help texts are byte-identical, one expect file per task (4 files) services both. Otherwise 8. Recommend identical (also forces parity). But careful: the programs are separate; keeping texts identical is a manual discipline. A shared expect file enforces it via tests. Good.\n\nNow, let me also consider `--help` output must end with newline? The expect normalization strips trailing LFs, so trailing newline differences don't matter. Good.\n\nNow, \"указание на --help\" — if added, every usage error message gets \" (см. --help)\" or similar. That changes many expect files (error expects), so a bigger test-expect update. Need to weigh. I'll ask.\n\nAlso: does help belong for `--help` only, or also `-h`? Include `-h`.\n\nAlso: what about `-v` for version? Layer 10 lists `--version`; course says `--help`/`-h`. Not `-v`. Keep version as `--version` only (already).\n\nNow let me also verify there's no existing `--help`/usage in any main. Grep showed none. Good.\n\nLet me also check: t3 programs read stdin; `--help` at top returns before reading — good.\n\nNow, one more architectural consideration: the \"single-channel\" contract we just established. Help → stdout, exit 0 → content channel. Consistent. If help → stderr with exit 0, it would break the `_stderr_empty` test for the help case. So stdout is strongly preferred given the new contract. Good argument.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `--help` should exit 0. Convention yes. The mentor's matrix says exit 0. Good.\n\nNow the plan structure. Also need to wire tests:\n- add `HELP_ARG \"--help\"` (and maybe `HELP_ARG_SHORT \"-h\"`) in cases.cmake.\n- 8 add_case entries (or more if testing -h): e.g. `add_case(t1_c_help t1_c \"${HELP_ARG}\" t1_nul \"${SUCCESS}\" EQ t1_help \"${OUT}\")`.\n- 4 expect files (t1_help..t4_help) if C/C++ identical; 8 otherwise.\n- Possibly `-h` tests: 8 more. Could cover `-h` for one task only or all. Decision.\n\nNow let me write the plan and questions. Since the user asked two things (1. implement, 2. what else missing), I'll:\n- Answer part 2 first (status table).\n- Present the plan for --help with the architectural axes and ask the decisions.\n\nLet me keep it structured but not overly long.\n\nLet me define the usage/help content proposal. For each task, help should include:\n- program name/purpose\n- usage line\n- argument description (for t4: R; for t1/t2/t3: reads stdin)\n- maybe exit codes\n- `--help`, `--version`\n\nExample for t3:\n```\nt3_bbox_c — bounding box и центроид облака точек\nИспользование: t3_bbox_c [--help] [--version]\nЧитает точки \"x y z\" из stdin до EOF, выводит количество, границы, центроид,\nсреднее расстояние.\nКоды возврата: 0 успех; 65 данные; 66 пустой ввод; 74 сбой ввода-вывода.\n```\n\nThat's content the user authors. I'll leave the exact wording to them, just propose structure.\n\nNow, one question: should each program's help mention its exit codes? Nice but optional.\n\nLet me now also confirm the other layer-10 item \"pipe-friendly\" — yes.\n\nLet me write the final answer with:\n1. Status of layer 10 (what's present, what's missing): table.\n2. Plan for --help (files, structure, tests, verification).\n3. Decisions to confirm (questions).\n\nI'll use the question tool for the key decisions.\n\nLet me carefully phrase the questions (max 4). Key ones:\nQ1. Module structure: extract common/cli (move version) vs keep in string_utils.\nQ2. Scope: only --help/-h vs also \"see --help\" hints in error messages.\nQ3. Help output stream: stdout (recommended) vs stderr.\nQ4. C/C++ help parity & expect files: identical shared expect vs separate.\n\nAlso `-h` inclusion could be folded into Q2 or a separate. Let me include `-h` in the plan by default (course mentions it) and ask if they want it. Actually just include both `--help` and `-h`; mention it.\n\nLet me finalize.\n\nActually, before asking, I should double-check one thing: does the course require the help to be Russian? The tasks output Russian. Help is user-facing → Russian, consistent. I'll state that as assumption.\n\nAlso encoding: help text source in CP1251 (like other literals), expect files CP1251. Note it.\n\nLet me now write.\n\nLet me also mention verification: cmake build, ctest full; negative control? For help, the `--help` test itself is positive. Could verify `-h`. The non-stream contract auto-covers stderr empty.\n\nAlso add README document `--help`.\n\nLet me also mention the interaction with `--version`: order of checks. Recommend `--version` then `--help` (or combine). If both given with extra args, argc!=2 → neither triggers → usage error. Convention nuance; keep argc==2 gate like version for simplicity, OR let help take precedence. I'll note as a minor decision folded into Q1/plan.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nI'll present the plan and then ask questions. Keep the plan concise but complete.\n\nLet me draft the status list precisely:\n\nLayer 10 (§5) = \"UX и справка CLI: argc/argv, --help, --version, разделение stdout/stderr, дифференцированные exit-коды, pipe-friendly\".\nPresent:\n- argc/argv — ✓ во всех t1–t4 (c/c++) + gen_cloud.\n- --version — ✓ (handle_version_flag, stdout, exit 0), тесты `_version`.\n- разделение stdout/stderr — ✓.\n- дифференцированные exit-коды (64/65/66/74) — ✓ (common/exit_codes.h/.hpp).\n- pipe-friendly — ✓.\nMissing:\n- --help (и usage-строка) — ✗.\n- -h — ✗.\n- \"указание на --help\" в ошибках — ✗ (§2.1.A criterion).\n\nThat's the answer to part 2.\n\nNow the plan.\n\nLet me write.",
