resent this transparently: 284 and 291 conflict; I recommend the standard resolution (291 + convention); note 284's wording is imprecise/possibly refers to usage-error help; and flag that if the mentor literally wants help on stderr, that would (a) deviate from convention and (b) complicate the single-channel contract/tests. Could ask the user to confirm with the mentor if unsure.\n\nActually — the user is the student; the mentor's material is authoritative. But materials conflict internally. Best: point out the conflict precisely, recommend the convention-based resolution, and suggest if in doubt, ask the mentor. That's honest.\n\nLet me also double-check: is there anywhere else in 02-praktika that clarifies? Line 373: \"stderr + exit code != 0. stdout остаётся чистым для рабочего вывода\". And line 284 criterion \"почему usage идёт в stderr\". So the course is firm that *usage on error* → stderr. And 291 says *--help* → stdout. So the standard split is strongly supported.\n\nSo my answer: \n- 284's \"справка в stderr\"/\"std::cerr\" conflicts with 291's \"--help → stdout\". \n- The consistent reading (and convention): explicit --help → stdout/0; usage/help-on-error → stderr/nonzero.\n- Recommend that. Note the test contract benefit.\n- If the mentor literally means help on stderr, flag risk; suggest verifying.\n\nNow, let me also address the user's phrase: \"если --help один единственный, то он напечатает в stderr но завершится с кодом 0?\" — answer: no; under recommended model, stdout/0. Under a literal 284 reading you'd get stderr/0 — but that's unusual and breaks the contract; not recommended.\n\nLet me structure the answer:\n\n1. Q1: exit-code taxonomy; hint belongs to \"invocation wrong\" (64). Explain 65/74 no hint. Nuance: t1/t2 classify some stdin-data cases as 64; attaching to 64 means hint there too — acceptable/consistent, though conceptually the hint is about CLI misuse.\n2. Q2: disentangle help output vs hint. Table. --help → stdout, exit 0, always. Hint → stderr, exit 64 (few words, part of diagnostic). No dual-channel. Correct their assumption explicitly.\n3. Q3: yes there are contradictions; quote 284 vs 291; recommend standard resolution; note test-contract implication; suggest confirming with mentor if strict adherence desired.\n\nKeep concise but clear. Use categories. No edits.\n\nLet me write.\n\nRegarding the hint text and whether the help command itself mentions usage: not needed.\n\nOne more nuance for Q2: The hint is a pointer like \"См. --help.\" It's not the help. So when error 64 occurs, output is: the specific message + \"См. --help.\" to stderr; exit 64. When user runs `prog --help`, output is full help to stdout; exit 0. Two different executions. No single execution writes both channels.\n\nAlso mention: in the error case, stdout stays empty (contract holds). In --help case, stderr stays empty. Good.\n\nLet me finalize.\n\nI should also correct my own earlier loose statement if I overstated 284. I did say earlier \"02-praktika.md:284 ... 'вывод справки через std::cerr' → это разные вещи. Разрешается так: help по явному запросу → stdout/0; usage при ошибке → stderr/64.\" That's the resolution I already gave. Now user asks to confirm. I'll reaffirm but be explicit that 284's tech-column wording literally says cerr, so it's a real textual conflict, resolved by convention + 291.\n\nGood.\n\nLet me write.",
