          "text": "Now I know the linking:\n- t1 uses `common_cpp_static` / `common_c_static` (geometry + string_utils).\n- t2 uses `string_utils_cpp_static` / `string_utils_c_static` (string_utils only).\n- t3/t4 use `common_cpp_shared` / `common_c_shared` (geometry + string_utils).\n\nSo `handle_version_flag` currently lives in string_utils, which is in all these libs. If we move it to `cli`, we must add cli.c/cli.cpp to ALL these targets:\n- common_cpp_static, common_c_static (t1)\n- string_utils_cpp_static, string_utils_c_static (t2)\n- common_cpp_shared, common_c_shared (t3/t4)\nThat's 6 target additions. Matches the plan.\n\nNote: there's also `geometry` in some, but cli isn't geometry.\n\nAlternatively, create a NEW library `cli_cpp`/`cli_c` and link it to all tasks. But that adds link dependencies to add_task calls (8 places). The plan said add cli to the 6 targets — simpler, keeps add_task unchanged. Good. But it means cli.cpp gets compiled into multiple libs (duplication of object in static libs is fine; in shared lib it's once). Actually, for static libs, cli.obj would be included in common_cpp_static AND string_utils_cpp_static → two copies, but each linked into different exes, so no ODR issue across exes. Fine.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: `handle_version_flag` currently is in string_utils, which t2's string_utils_cpp_static also contains. If we move it out and add cli to string_utils_cpp_static, fine.\n\nNow, the plan is solid. Remaining clarifications:\n1. **Hint scope** — the \"8 places\" concern. After 64→65, only t4 has usage errors. Confirm: hint only in t4? Or also on t1/t2/t3 data errors?\n2. **Help content** — do they write the 4 texts or want a template? And should help include version/exit codes?\n3. **`-h` tests** — separate cases for all 8, or fewer?\n4. **Hint wording/placement** — exact text and whether it's a new line.\n\nAlso technical: the usage/help text must be identical C/C++ (shared expect). Confirm.\n\nAlso: should `handle_help