          "text": "The user asks two questions:\n1. Should the `const char* help_text` be organized as they're doing (defining a text variable right after main / at top, and passing it)? What's the idiomatic way?\n2. What does \"Использование: [--help] [--version]\" mean inside the help text — does it mean \"how to find out version / how to show help\"?\n\nLet me answer both.\n\nFor (1): The question is about how to organize the help_text literal in each main. Options:\n- `static const char* const HELP_TEXT = \"...\";` at file scope (near top), then pass to handle_help_flag.\n- Pass a string literal directly at the call site: `handle_help_flag(argc, argv, \"....\")`.\n- For C++: `constexpr std::string_view`.\n\nThey say \"сразу после main делаю проверку и вот это текст вывожу\" — meaning inside main, right after (or before?) the version check, they call handle_help_flag with the text. And they ask whether the `const char*` way is right.\n\nI should explain:\n- If the text is passed directly as a literal at the call site, no variable needed: `if (handle_help_flag(argc, argv, \"…текст…\")) return 0;`. That's simplest. But a multi-line help literal inline in the middle of main is ugly. Better: define it as a file-scope constant (or a local static) and pass it.\n- C: `static const char HELP_TEXT[] = \"...\";` at file scope (above main). `const char*` is fine. Use `[]` array or pointer; `static const char HELP_TEXT[]` is idiomatic for a string constant. Passing it as `const char*` works (array decays).\n- C++: `constexpr std::string_view kHelpText = R\"(...)\";` at file scope (anonymous namespace preferred? but it's file-local, so `namespace { constexpr std::string_view kHelpText = ...; }` or just `constexpr` at file scope with internal linkage—`constexpr` implies const → internal linkage at namespace scope in C++). Actually `constexpr` variables at namespace scope have internal linkage by default (const → internal), so no ODR issue. Fine.\n- Multi-line: raw string literal `R\"(...)\"` is convenient in C++ but the file is CP1251 and raw strings are fine. In C, use adjacent string literals `\"line1\\n\" \"line2\\n\"` or `\\n` inside one literal.\n\nAlso, the help text should be defined so C and C++ versions are byte-identical (shared expect). So the literal content must match exactly.\n\nAlso: `static` in C for a file-scope constant gives internal linkage (avoids symbol clash). Good.\n\nAlso, where to place: file scope above main (after includes). Not \"right after main\" (that phrasing is odd). I'll clarify: place it at file scope before main, or as a local `const` inside main (but then it's re-created each call—negligible). Idiomatic: file-scope constant.\n\nAlso, naming: `HELP_TEXT` (C style) or `kHelpText` (C++). The project uses... let me recall: they use `kVersion`, camel? In journal: `kVersion`. In code, they have `INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION`. Function-level naming uses lower_snake. Constants? `kExecFailed` in run_case.cpp (k-prefix). So C++ uses `k`-prefix. C uses? `MAX_SIZE`/`MIN_SIZE` macros in t1_c. Hmm. For a const string, C could use `const char HELP_TEXT[]`.\n\nSo answer: yes, `const char* help_text` param is fine; organize the literal as a file-scope `static const char HELP_TEXT[] = \"...\";` (C) or `constexpr std::string_view kHelpText = R\"(...)\";` (C++), and pass it. Not inline in the middle of main.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety with `const char*` vs `const char[]`: if they write `static const char* HELP_TEXT = \"...\";`, that's a pointer to a literal — fine, but the pointer itself could be reassigned (not const). Better `static const char HELP_TEXT[] = \"...\";` or `static const char* const HELP_TEXT = \"...\";`. For passing to `const char*` param, both work.\n\nActually, simplest and version.h-adjacent: no strong need for `static` if it's only used in main? At file scope, without static, C gives external linkage → potential clash if two TUs define `HELP_TEXT`? Each main is a separate exe/TU, so no clash. But `static` is good hygiene. I'll recommend `static`.\n\nFor (2): \"Использование: [--help] [--version]\" — the user asks what it means. It's the usage/synopsis line: how the program is invoked. For t1-t3 (which read only stdin), the only command-line arguments are optional `--help` and `--version`; so \"Использование: [--help] [--version]\" means \"invoke with no arguments (reads stdin); optionally --help or --version\". For t4: \"Использование: <R> [--help] [--version]\" means one required argument R, plus optional --help/--version.\n\nSo it does mean \"how to invoke the program, including how to request help/version\". It's the standard synopsis. The `[...]` denotes optional. `<R>` denotes required placeholder. This is POSIX/GNU convention (the course links the POSIX utility conventions).\n\nI should clarify the notation:\n- `[...]` = optional.\n- `<...>` or bare name = required operand.\n- The program name usually appears (`prog [options]`), but we omit it (static parity) — explain.\n\nAlso clarify: the line doesn't mean \"how to find out version\" per se; it lists the CLI options the program accepts. `--version` prints version; `--help` prints this help. It's the synopsis.\n\nAlso, I could point out that for t1-t3, since they read stdin, the help should mention that input comes from stdin — which the drafts do.\n\nNow, the user says \"вот например t1_c, сразу после main делаю проверку и вот это текст вывожу\" — they may be asking whether the help text should be a variable defined and passed, and whether the synopsis makes sense.\n\nLet me answer concretely with a small example for t1_c organization.\n\nSince plan mode, no edits; just explain and maybe show a snippet (as text).\n\nLet me also flag: the help_text passed to the function — the newline policy (from previous review) still pending. And the function adds `\\n` per the corrected printf/println. So the literal should NOT end with `\\n`.\n\nLet me also confirm: for C, if they define `static const char HELP_TEXT[