ally if the axis were \"value problem → 65\", then radius value → 65 AND t1 range → 65, and only missing/extra args → 64. That's also self-consistent! Let me consider.\n\nTwo self-consistent models:\nModel A (channel-based):\n- argv presence/shape errors AND argv value errors → 64.\n- stdin value errors → 65.\nSo: t4 missing/extra → 64; t4 bad radius value → 64; t1 range → 65; t2 negative → 65.\n\nModel B (nature-based):\n- \"invocation structure wrong\" (missing/extra/unknown option) → 64.\n- \"provided value wrong (whether argv or stdin)\" → 65.\nSo: t4 missing/extra → 64; t4 bad radius value → 65; t1 range → 65; t2 negative → 65.\n\nHmm! Model B is also self-consistent and matches the user's intuition: any \"the value you gave is bad\" → 65, regardless of channel. And \"structure of invocation\" → 64.\n\nWhich does the course/sysexits support? sysexits:\n- EX_USAGE (64): \"The command was used incorrectly, e.g., with the wrong number of arguments, a bad flag, a bad syntax in a parameter, or whatever.\" → \"bad syntax in a parameter\" is explicitly 64. So a malformed radius (\"abc\") → 64. An out-of-range but syntactically valid radius? \"bad syntax\" arguably not; but \"used incorrectly\" could cover. Hmm. The standard example: EX_USAGE for \"bad syntax in a parameter\". So \"abc\" (not a number) → syntax error → 64. \"-10\" (valid number, invalid domain) → is that \"syntax\" or \"data\"? Ambiguous. Could be 65 (data) or 64 (usage). Debatable.\n\nGNU convention: `--block-size=xyz` → usage error; but a numeric option value out of range → often usage error too (e.g., `head -n -5`?). Generally option-argument errors are usage.\n\nPractically, the project's own earlier decision (2026-07-13 journal): \"для аргумента код 64, для данных в потоке - 65.\" — argument → 64. So the project already uses channel-based (Model A). And it reclassified t1/t2 stdin values to 65. So Model A is the established, consistent model.\n\nSo I should explain both models, note that the project already picked channel-based (Model A), and that under it, radius-value errors are 64 (argv). And sysexits EX_USAGE explicitly names \"bad syntax in a parameter\". So keep 64 + hint.\n\nBut I should be honest: the user's intuition maps to Model B, which is also defensible; but switching to B now would mean also treating \"radius syntactically bad\" as 65, which conflicts with the project's own rule (argument → 64) and the fact that we JUST moved t1/t2 to 65 *because they're stdin*. If we adopted B, the distinguishing factor becomes \"value bad vs structure bad\", and then t1/t2 would ALSO be 65 (they are), and t4 radius value would be 65. Hmm, actually B and the current state differ only on t4 radius values.\n\nLet me lay it out clearly and recommend A (keep 64 for radius), because:\n- Project rule: argument → 64.\n- sysexits EX_USAGE covers \"bad syntax in a parameter\".\n- The hint is exactly for argv-validation errors.\n- It keeps \"--help hint\" tied to \"you typed the command wrong\", which is the GNU norm.\n\nAnd note: Model B would split argv errors into two subclasses (structure vs value), which is finer-grained and not required by the course; it also makes 64 nearly empty (only missing/extra). The course criterion \"при неверном вводе — указание на --help\" fits argv errors (both structure and value).\n\nSo answer: you're half-right (it's a value problem), but by the project's and sysexits' convention, argv-parameter errors — including bad values — are 64. Keep the hint. The 65 is reserved for stream/stdin data.\n\nLet me also confirm the existing t4 behavior: all four radius value errors → usage (64). Yes. So keeping is consistent.\n\nLet me write concise but with the reasoning and the two models.\n\nThe user asked \"или ошибаюсь?\" — answer: your intuition is conceptually reasonable but the convention says otherwise; keep 64.\n\nLet me write with categories and confidence, briefly.\n\nAlso I'm in plan mode — no edits. Just answer.\n\nLet me write.",
