 for CP1251). Let me note to verify round-trip.\n\nAlternatively, decode escapes at the byte level to avoid any decoding: operate on the raw byte array, extract between quotes, replace byte pairs `5C 6E`→`0A` and `5C 22`→`22`, concatenate. That's fully encoding-agnostic. Even better! I can propose the byte-level extraction (no text decoding at all). That's the safest.\n\nSo the execution step: byte-level extract from the C source and write expect bytes. I'll detail it.\n\nLet me also think: are there other escape sequences in the help literals besides `\\n` and `\\\"`? Let me check: t1-t4 help texts: t1 no quotes; t2 has `«»` (no escapes); t3 has `\\\"x y z\\\"`; t4 has `\\\"x y z\\\"`. So only `\\n` and `\\\"`. Byte-level replace covers both. Also `\\\\`? No. Good.\n\nNow, one more: the C source has the literals split across lines with leading whitespace and `\\r\\n`. The extraction: find `HELP_TEXT[] =` then capture all `\"...\"` until `;`. Byte-level regex on the byte array? Easier to convert the ASCII structure to string? The bytes include CP1251 in the literals, but the structure (quotes, `\\n`, semicolon) is ASCII. I can do byte-level scanning: find index of `HELP_TEXT[] =`, then scan for `\"`...`\"` segments, respecting `\\\"` (escaped quote). Collect bytes inside, decoding escapes. Write.\n\nThis is a bit of code but straightforward.\n\nLet me present the plan and offer the creation method. Also ask if they want me to author expects from literals vs run-capture (recommend literals).\n\nNow, the plan should also include: README update (the user earlier deferred; now help exists), CHANGELOG, journal. And the note that `--help` should be added to README CLI docs.\n\nLet me present the plan concisely.\n\nAlso, test count: 1081 + 48 = 1129. But wait — is the current count 1081? Let me re-verify: the last full Windows run reported 1081 (after the coverage additions). Then we added only expects/code, no add_case. So 1081. +16×3=48 → 1129.\n\nHmm, actually let me double check the current number of add_case and the fact that help adds 16 cases. Also, the `--help` cases use t1_nul which exists.\n\nLet me also confirm `HELP_ARG`/`HELP_SHORT_ARG` names don't collide. New.\n\nNow, one subtlety: the `_short` test name. `t1_c_help_short` — fine.\n\nLet me also consider: do we want a negative control? Yes: temporarily break one help literal (e.g., t1 C) → `t1_c_help` and `t1_c_help_short` fail (C), while `t1_cpp_help` stays green (since C++ still correct) → demonstrates the C/C++ parity check. Good.\n\nLet me present.\n\nAlso: the plan should mention that after adding, `cmake --preset debug` (reconfigure to pick up cases.cmake) + build + ctest.\n\nLet me write the plan.\n\nQuestions to ask:\n1. Creation method for the 4 expects: byte-extract from C literals (recommended, independent) vs capture from program.\n2. Confirm 16 cases (both flags for all 8 programs) — already decided (full tests for -h). Good, no need.\n3. README/CHANGELOG now? (User may want.) I'll include.\n\nActually the user already decided \"-h полные тесты для всех\". So just confirm method.\n\nLet me present the plan with the exact cases.cmake block, and ask the one question about expect creation.\n\nLet me write concisely.\n\nI should show the exact add_case lines they can copy (they earlier asked for copy-paste). Let me provide them.\n\nLet me write.",
