          "text": "The user clarifies: Q1 is not about version; they meant a **manual run and capturing the errorlevel**, like they did for `check.exe` (feeding wrong arguments and checking `echo %errorlevel%`). They're asking whether it makes sense to do such a manual probe for `--help`/`-h`.\n\nSo: should they do a manual probe for help flags — feed wrong/near-miss arguments and check the exit code/errorlevel?\n\nMy answer: yes, a manual probe makes sense (cheap, read-only-ish), and here's what to probe and what to expect. But note the behavior nuances:\n- `prog --help` / `-h` → exit 0, help to stdout.\n- `prog --helpp` (typo) → not recognized → for t1-t3: ignored, reads stdin (empty) → 66; for t4: argc==2 → parse_radius(\"--helpp\") → not_number → 64 + hint.\n- `prog --help extra` → argc==3 → not help. t1-t3: ignored, read stdin → 66; t4: argc!=2 → 64 + hint.\n- `prog -v` → not recognized (only `--version`), not `-h`. t1-t3: ignored → 66; t4: 64+hint.\n- `prog -h` → exit 0.\n\nSo the manual probe would show: help/-h → 0; anything else → normal path (which for t1-t3 is input-dependent). This confirms the `argc==2` gate and that only exact flags trigger.\n\nThe question: is it worth doing? Yes, as a one-time manual check (like the check.exe probe), to confirm the gate and that `-h`/`--help` are exact. It's cheap evidence for §4.9.7 (branches exercised). Not automated.\n\nBut caution: for t1-t3, feeding `--helpp` with empty stdin gives 66 — that's the normal no-input path, which may look confusing in the journal (\"why 66?\"). Worth noting.\n\nSo I'll recommend a small manual probe with expected results, and note it goes in the journal (like the check.exe probe).\n\nLet me present the plan for the manual probe:\n- Use cmd (not PowerShell) to capture `%errorlevel%`, like they did for check.exe.\n- Cases (on t4, since it has usage errors, and on t1 to show the \"ignored arg\" behavior):\n  1. `t4_filter_c\\main.exe --help` → stdout help, exit 0.\n  2. `t4_filter_c\\main.exe -h` → exit 0.\n  3. `t4_filter_c\\main.exe --helpp` → exit 64, stderr message + hint.\n  4. `t4_filter_c\\main.exe --help extra` → exit 64 (+hint).\n  5. `t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe --help` → exit 0.\n  6. `t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe --helpp` (empty stdin) → exit 66, stderr \"нет N\".\n  7. `t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe -v` → exit 66 (ignored → read stdin).\n- Remember: from plain cmd you need the DLL in PATH (`set PATH=build\\debug\\common;%PATH%`) for t3/t4.\n\nActually the user just wants confirmation/advice. Since plan mode, I present the plan (what to run, expected), not run it.\n\nAlso Q2 (delete gtest_smoke) — they didn't answer explicitly; the message is only about Q1. I should re-ask/confirm Q2? The user's message only addresses Q1. I'll note Q2 still open (my recommendation: yes delete) and ask to confirm.\n\nActually, re-reading: they wrote two \"1.\" lines (duplicate). Both about Q1. No mention of Q2. So Q2 (gtest_smoke deletion) is still awaiting their decision. I'll briefly ask to confirm.\n\nLet me answer concisely:\n- Yes, manual probe makes sense (cheap, matches the check.exe approach). Give the probe list + expected codes.\n- Note the nuance for t1-t3 (ignored args → normal stdin path → 66), which is arguably a separate UX gap.\n- Confirm Q2 recommendation and ask.\n\nLet me keep it concise and clear, per plan mode.\n\nLet me write.\n\nExpected results table:\n\n| команда | ожидание |\n|---|---|\n| t4 --help | выход help в stdout, exit 0 |\n| t4 -h | то же, exit 0 |\n| t4 --helpp | exit 64, stderr: «Радиус должен быть числом…» + хинт |\n| t4 --help extra | exit 64, stderr: «Ожидался радиус; были введены лишние аргументы» + хинт |\n| t1 --help | help в stdout, exit 0 |\n| t1 --helpp (пустой stdin) | exit 66, stderr «Введите число N» (аргумент проигнорирован) |\n| t1 -v (пустой stdin) | exit 66 (то же) |\n\nNote t4 `--help extra`: argc==3 → `argc != 2` branch → message \"Ожидался радиус; были введены лишние аргументы\" + hint → 64. Good.\n\nAlso