          "text": "The user wants:\n1. Q3: agreed — no experiment; keep `<iostream>` (no transitive reliance, per course policy).\n2. Q2: \"проверь ридми и changelog - всё ли верно я занесла?\" — they say they've added the docs. I need to read README and CHANGELOG to verify.\n3. Q1: variant B — strict argument validation. They ask if the approach is: after the two ifs (version/help), add another branch for the arg — is it `return usage` with hint? \"логично?\"\n\nI'm in plan mode, read-only. Let me read README (around the help section and limitations) and CHANGELOG to verify.\n\nLet me read:\n- README around 213-260 (new \"Справка\" section + limitations).\n- CHANGELOG head.\n\nLet me also check git status to see if they committed.\n\nThen answer Q1 with the plan for strict validation.\n\nFor Q1, let me think about the correct implementation approach.\n\nCurrent t1 main:\n```c\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    if (handle_version_flag(argc, argv)) return 0;\n    if (handle_help_flag(argc, argv, HELP_TEXT)) return 0;\n    // then reads N from stdin\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nThe user proposes: after the two ifs, add a branch checking the argument and `return usage` with hint.\n\nBut careful: `handle_version_flag`/`handle_help_flag` only handle `argc==2` with the exact recognized flag. After them:\n- `argc == 1` (no args) → normal path (should proceed).\n- `argc == 2` with an unrecognized arg (e.g., `--helpp`, `-v`, `foo`) → not handled by the two ifs → should error.\n- `argc >= 3` (e.g., `--help extra`, `--version extra`, two junk args) → not handled → should error.\n\nSo the check should be: `if (argc != 1) → usage error + hint; return usage;` placed after the two ifs. Because at that point, any args present are unrecognized (the recognized ones already returned).\n\nWait: is that right? The two ifs return for valid cases when argc==2. So if we reach the check with argc>1, it means args are unrecognized/extra → error. So `if (argc > 1) → error`. Equivalent to `argc != 1`.\n\nBut hold on: For t4, the main structure is different: it has argc validation already (argc != 2 → usage). But t4 also has the help/version ifs first. For `t4 --help` argc==2 → help returns. `t4 --version` → version returns. `t4` (no arg) → argc==1 → `argc != 2` → usage (missing radius). `t4 foo` → argc==2 → parse_radius(\"foo\") → error. `t4 --help extra` → argc==3 → `argc != 2` → usage \"лишние аргументы\". So t4 already rejects unknown/extra args (via its radius logic). So t4 is fine; only t1–t3 lack validation.\n\nSo for t1–t3 (both C and C++): after version/help ifs, add:\n```c\nif (argc != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Неизвестный аргумент(ы); программа не принимает аргументов. Передано: ...\\n\");\n    print_help_hint();\n    return usage;\n}\n```\nWait, but should we include the hint? The hint is for usage errors. t1–t3 currently have no hint. If we add usage error, adding the hint is consistent (usage → hint). But the hint text says \"Для справки вызовите программу с параметром --help или -h\" — appropriate.\n\nBut note: `print_help_hint` is in `common/cli`, declared in cli.h/.hpp. t1–t3 mains include cli.h/.hpp already (for version/help). So accessible. Good.\n\nMessage style (§4.6): specific — state that args aren't expected and what was received, e.g.:\n\"Программа не принимает аргументов. Получено: <argv[1]>\" or if multiple, list. Hmm. But careful with §4.6 \"без штампов\". Something like: \"Неизвестный аргумент: <argv[1]>\". Or \"Ожидался вызов без аргументов; получено N аргумент(ов): ...\".\n\nActually, we need to decide the message. And the expect file. And whether to test.\n\nAlso: C and C++ parity (shared expect).\n\nAlso: what about `--version extra` (argc=3)? → not handled by version (gate argc==2) → reaches `argc != 1` → usage error. Good. And `--help extra` → same. Good.\n\nWait, but is it desirable that `prog --help extra` errors instead of showing help? GNU would show help. But our design: strict argc. The user wants strict. Fine, consistent with own design.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: `handle_help_flag` checks `argc == 2`. So `prog --help extra` → false → falls to the strict check → usage error. That's the chosen contract.\n\nNow, the user asks: \"надо после 2ух if на то, version / help Ли этот аргумент сделать ещё ветку и return usage с хинтом. логично?\"\n\nAnswer: Almost — the check isn't \"is the arg version/help\" (that's already done by the two ifs); it's \"are there ANY unexpected args left?\" Since after the two ifs, any argc != 1 means unexpected. So:\n```c\nif (handle_version_flag(...)) return 0;\nif (handle_help_flag(...)) return 0;\nif (argc != 1) { /* unknown/extra arg */ return usage; }\n```\nSo: yes, a branch after the two ifs, `return usage`, with hint. But phrase it as \"argc != 1\" (not \"the arg isn't version/help\"). Logical.\n\nAlso mention: t4 doesn't need this (it already rejects via argc!=2). Only t1–t3.\n\nAlso: tests — add for t1_c/t1_cpp, t2_c/t2_cpp, t3_c/t3_cpp: a case with an unknown arg (e.g., `--foo`) → usage(64), stderr message+hint, stdout empty. And maybe `--help extra`. Need input (t1_nul). And a new expect per language or shared? The message must be identical C/C++ per task → shared expect per task (3 files: t1_arg, t2_arg, t3_arg) if texts identical. But the message may differ per task? We can make it identical generic: \"Программа не принимает аргументов. Получено: --foo\". Hmm, if identical across t1/t2/t3, one expect could serve all? But the received arg differs. Let me use the same arg for the test across tasks, and same message → could share one expect. But the message might include the program? No. So a single generic message + same test arg → one expect for all three tasks (both C and C++). That's economical. But careful: is the message identical for t1/t2/t3? If we author it identically, yes.\n\nHmm, but the hint + message + received arg: e.g.:\n```\nНеизвестный аргумент: --foo\nДля справки вызовите программу с параметром --help или -h\n```\nThe `--foo` is the same in the test for all tasks. So one shared expect `t_arg_unknown` could serve t1/t2/t3 (c/cpp) = 6 cases. Plus maybe a second case for extra args (`--help extra`)? The message would list args. Keep it simple: one case per task with a single unknown arg.\n\nWait, but if the message is generic and the test arg is the same, a single expect works for all 6 cases. But is that good practice? It's fine (like t1_help shared by t1_c/t1_cpp).\n\nActually, hold on: should the message include the received arg? §4.6 says state what was received. So \"Получено: --foo\". Since the test uses `--foo` for all, one expect.\n\nBut actually, different tasks might have different allowed args conceptually, but the message is generic. So yes, one shared expect.\n\nAlternatively, keep it per-task to be safe. Minor.\n\nAlso: the hint. If we add hint to t1–t3 usage errors, that's consistent. But wait — earlier decision was \"hint only on 64\". t1–t3 now get a 64 (unknown arg) → hint appropriate. Good, consistent.\n\nNow, is this change within the \"help\" feature or separate? The user chose (B) as a new task now. OK.\n\nImpact summary:\n- 6 mains (t1/t2/t3 × c/cpp): add `if (argc != 1) { msg; print_help_hint(); return usage; }` after version/help ifs.\n- New expect(s).\n- New add_case(s) in cases.cmake.\n- Update README (help section mentions strict args?), CHANGELOG.\n- Tests.\n\nAlso consider: t1–t3 read stdin; the new check happens before stdin read, so with unknown arg it errors immediately (no stdin read). Good. Test input can be t1_nul.\n\nAlso: what message exactly? Let me propose:\nC: `fprintf(stderr, \"Программа не принимает аргументов. Получено: %s\\n\", argv[1]);` then `print_help_hint();`\nBut if argc>2, argv[1] is just the first; maybe list all. For simplicity and determinism, since our gate is argc!=1, and the test uses a single arg, we can print argv[1]. But if user passes two args, printing only the first is incomplete. Better: print count and/or list. Hmm. To keep it simple: `\"Неизвестный аргумент: %s\"` for argc==2, and for argc>2 something else. But that's two branches (complexity). \n\nAlternative: use the \"argv listing\" approach like in check.cpp (loop). But that adds a loop. Hmm.\n\nActually, simplest robust: \n```\nif (argc != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Программа не принимает аргументов. Получено %d аргумент(ов):\", argc - 1);\n    for (int i = 1; i < argc; ++i) fprintf(stderr, \" [%s]\", argv[i]);\n    fprintf(stderr, \"\\n\");\n    print_help_hint();\n    return usage;\n}\n```\nThat's a bit heavy but robust. Or simpler: just `\"Ожидался вызов без аргументов; получено: %s\\n\", argv[1]` — acceptable for the likely case.\n\nGiven §4.6 wants specificity and the user's style, I'd propose listing all args (like check.cpp does). But that's more code in each main (6 places) — duplication. Hmm. Could add a helper in cli: `print_unexpected_args(argc, argv)`? That centralizes. But it's a new API. Alternatively, keep the message simple with argv[1] since our design realistically only sees 1-2 args.\n\nHmm, actually — reconsider: the cleanest might be to reject at argc != 1 with a generic message that names the first unexpected arg:\n`\"Ожидался вызов без аргументов. Получен аргумент: %s\"` (for the single-arg case). For multiple, it'd still show argv[1]. Given tests use single, fine. But not fully general.\n\nI'll present options and let the user decide, but recommend a small helper or the listing loop. Actually, to avoid duplication across 6 mains, a cli helper is attractive:\n- `print_unexpected_args_hint(argc, argv)`? Hmm, but the diagnostic should be in the task (it's task-level). Eh.\n\nLet me propose a minimal approach and note the alternative:\nOption 1 (simple): `fprintf(stderr, \"Ожидался вызов без аргументов. Получено: %s\\n\", argv[1]); print_help_hint(); return usage;`\nOption 2 (robust): loop listing all args.\n\nRecommend Option 1 for simplicity now (tests cover single arg), note that if multiple args matter, use the loop.\n\nAlso, the user asked \"логично?\" — yes, logically correct, with the refinement that the condition is `argc != 1` (after the two ifs), not \"check if it's version/help\".\n\nNow, Q2: verify README/CHANGELOG they added. Let me read README around the new section and CHANGELOG. Let me read.\n\nLet me read README lines 205-270 and CHANGELOG (full or head).\n\nLet me run reads.\n\nLet me also check git status (did they commit?).\n\nLet me do it.",
