EQ t_arg_unknown_multi \"${ERR}\")\nadd_case(t3_cpp_arg_unknown_multi t3_cpp \"${UNKNOWN_ARGS}\" t1_nul \"${USAGE}\" EQ t_arg_unknown_multi \"${ERR}\")\n```\n\nAnd the `set` lines (near HELP_ARG):\n```cmake\nset(UNKNOWN_ARG \"--foo\")\nset(UNKNOWN_ARGS --foo bar)\n```\n\nWait: `set(UNKNOWN_ARG \"--foo\")` → single string. `set(UNKNOWN_ARGS --foo bar)` → list of two.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: `UNKNOWN_ARGS --foo bar` → CMake list `--foo;bar`. Quoted `\"${UNKNOWN_ARGS}\"` → \"--foo;bar\" as one arg to add_case → then add_test unquoted → two args. Good.\n\nBut wait: `UNKNOWN_ARG \"--foo\"` — is `--foo` interpreted as an option by set? No, set treats it as a value. Fine. Actually `set(UNKNOWN_ARG \"--foo\")` — the quotes prevent `--foo` from being treated as a keyword. Good. Without quotes, `set(UNKNOWN_ARG --foo)` also works (set doesn't parse options after the variable name? Actually `set(<var> <value>...)` — value \"--foo\" is fine). But quoting is safer.\n\nHmm, actually there's a subtlety with `set(UNKNOWN_ARGS --foo bar)`: CMake might interpret `--foo`? No, set takes variable name then values. Fine.\n\nNow, I should present this. Note the count: 12 add_case → 36 tests (Windows 1128 → 1164; VM 1140 → 1176). Wait: 12 cases × 3 = 36. Windows 1128 + 36 = 1164; VM 1140 + 36 = 1176.\n\nHmm, earlier I said 6 cases (18 tests) for single; now with multi, 12 cases (36 tests).\n\nAlso note: placement — after the help tests section (end of cases.cmake).\n\nAlso: verify that `t1_nul` input works (program errors before reading stdin); yes.\n\nAlso: the `_code` test expects USAGE (64). `_stderr` compares to t_arg_unknown/_multi. `_stdout_empty` auto.\n\nLet me present concisely.\n\nAlso mention: the multiple-arg test relies on the CMake list expansion (same mechanism as RAD_TOO_MUCH), so quoting at the call is required.\n\nLet me write the answer. Since plan mode, just show the code block.\n\nI must not run non-readonly tools. Showing text is fine.\n\nLet me produce.",
