s closest to git's (error → stderr, nonzero), though git's reason differs.\n\nThe user's doubt: \"может при ошибке в самом help флаге stderr, а при --help smth_strange — stdout?\" i.e., distinguish \"unknown flag\" (stderr) from \"extra operand after valid --help\" (stdout?).\n\nLet me think about what's most principled and defensible:\n- Clean `--help`/`-h` alone → stdout, exit 0. (universal, clear)\n- Unknown/typo flag (e.g., `--helpp`) → not help; it's an unrecognized option → error → stderr, exit !=0. (universal among git/cmake/cl)\n- `--help <extra>`: depends on the tool's contract. Since our t1–t3 take NO operands and t4 takes exactly one radius operand (not after help), `--help <extra>` is \"misuse\" → error. Whether the diagnostic goes to stdout or stderr: it's an ERROR → stderr, exit !=0. There's no reason to send an error message to stdout.\n\nSo my recommendation: keep it simple and consistent with our single-channel contract:\n- help alone → stdout/0.\n- everything else (unknown flag, extra operand after help) → stderr/!=0 (usage 64 + hint).\n\nThis is defensible and matches the dominant pattern: errors → stderr. The `cl /help help` (stdout/0) is the outlier (cl is famously lenient). cmake's stdout-but-nonzero is a quirk.\n\nBut I should present this honestly, noting that behavior with extra args varies and there's no universal rule; the mentor said \"look at known utilities and imitate\". The most representative/common behavior for \"error\" is stderr + nonzero. And clean help → stdout/0.\n\nAlso, the \"hint\" (Try --help): do git/cmake/cl print a hint on error? The user observed git --helpp → \"error + hint\"; cmake --helpp → \"stderr+hint\". So hint on unknown-flag error is common. For `--help extra`, the hint would be less common (since the user already knows --help). Hmm. So maybe: hint only on unknown-flag errors, not on `--help extra`? That's a refinement. But our design prints hint on all usage errors. Minor.\n\nNow, the user asks: \"советуешь ли