oo` → ls prints help and exits 0, ignoring foo? Let me recall: GNU coreutils: `ls --help foo` → prints help, exit 0 I believe. Yes, GNU tools typically process --help and exit immediately, ignoring other operands.) Hmm! So GNU convention might actually be: `--help` short-circuits, ignores the rest, stdout/0. That would contradict our strict approach!\n\nWait, let me reconsider. GNU coreutils `ls --help foo`: I believe it prints help and exits 0 (help takes precedence). Actually, GNU getopt: --help is handled and the program calls usage() and exits 0 before processing operands. So extra operands are ignored. So `ls --help foo` → stdout/0.\n\nSimilarly `git --help foo` — but git is special (its own parser; --help foo = help for foo).\n\nHmm. So there's genuine divergence: GNU coreutils → help wins, exit 0, ignores extras; git → interprets as subcommand; cl → ignores extras, exit 0; cmake → keyword mode.\n\nSo if we're imitating \"known utilities\", a case could be made that `prog --help extra` → help to stdout, exit 0 (ignore extras), like GNU coreutils and cl. That would mean our strict rejection of `--help extra` is NOT what the majority does.\n\nHmm. This is important. The user's doubt is valid. Let me think about what to recommend.\n\nLet me check: does the user's own probe show GNU coreutils? They tested git, cmake, cl. Not coreutils. So they haven't seen GNU coreutils behavior.\n\nActually, let me reconsider `ls --help foo`. I'm fairly confident GNU coreutils print help and exit 0 regardless of extra args (because --help is checked early and exits). Let me not assert 100% without checking; I can suggest the user test it.\n\nHmm, but I can't run commands? Actually I can run read-only commands; running `ls --help foo` is read-only (prints help). But we're on Windows; `ls` is PowerShell alias, not GNU ls. Could test in the VM? Not accessible from here. So I can't verify GNU coreutils here. I'll flag as \"проверь сам\".\n\nSo the key decision for our project: should