dance points toward testing more utilities to decide.\n\nMy recommendation: \n- Present the analysis: the divergence root is whether `--help <arg>` is a defined mode in the tool.\n- For our tools (no subcommands, no `--help <topic>` mode), the closest \"known utility\" pattern is GNU coreutils / cl: `--help` prints help and exits 0, ignoring extra operands. If we follow that, change current strict behavior for `--help extra` to help-wins/stdout/0.\n- But: our t1–t3 reject any args; if we adopt \"help wins\", then `--help extra` → help (0), while `--foo` → error. Consistent enough.\n- Alternatively keep strict (current) — simpler test model, but less aligned with GNU.\n\nGiven the mentor explicitly told her to imitate known utilities, I lean: test GNU coreutils (`ls --help foo`, `grep --help foo`, `python --help foo`, `git help`?), and decide. My opinion: adopt \"help wins\" (GNU/cl) for `--help`/`-h` with extras → stdout/0; keep unknown flags → stderr/64.\n\nHmm, but that would mean t1–t3 need to change: currently `argc != 1` rejects `--help extra` (because handle_help_flag requires argc==2, then argc!=1 → error). To make help win, handle_help_flag should NOT gate on argc==2 but on \"argv[1] is --help/-h\" (ignore rest). And version similarly? For `--version extra` GNU → version/0? Probably yes (version wins too).\n\nThis is a real design change. Let me present it as an option and recommend, but note the tradeoff with the single-channel test contract (help→stdout/0 with extra args still fine for our tests).\n\nBut careful: does making `--help` win change what tests we need? We'd test `--help extra` → stdout/0 (help). And `--helpp`/`--foo` → 64+hint. Our existing `t_arg_unknown_multi` uses `--foo bar` → error, unaffected. We don't currently test `--help extra` as error (we test `--foo`). So changing `--help extra` to help-wins wouldn't break existing tests except... we don't have a `--help extra` test. So low impact.\n\nHmm wait, actually our current `handle_help_flag` gates `argc==2`. If we change to ignore extras, then `--help extra` → help. And our strict `argc != 1` check would then be reached only if argv[1] isn't help/version... but `--help extra` returns earlier. So the `argc != 1` check handles `--foo bar` (argv[1] unknown). Fine.\n\nBut then `--version extra` → version/0 (if version also ignores extras). Currently `handle_version_flag` gates argc==2. Should we relax both? For consistency, yes.\n\nThis is getting into a design decision the user should make, ideally after checking GNU coreutils behavior.\n\nSo my answer:\n1. Yes, we had the 284/291 contradiction; my position was help→stdout/0, hint→stderr/64 (as recapped).\n2. The mentor's guidance: imitate known utilities. Your probes show: clean help → stdout/0 (git, cmake, cl); unknown help flag → stderr/!=0 (all); extra after help → **varies** (cl ignores → stdout/0; git/cmake treat `--help <arg>` as a **defined mode** → error). So the divergence is not \"stdout vs stderr for the same thing\" but \"is `--help <arg>` a defined mode\".\n3. Correction of interpretation: git `--help me` isn't \"extra junk\"; it's `git help me` (help for command me), a defined form → hence stderr on failure. So git is NOT evidence for \"extra args → stderr\".\n4. The authoritative convention for tools WITHOUT a help-subcommand mode: `--help` wins, ignores other args, stdout/0 (GNU coreutils; cl behaves so). Recommend testing `ls --help foo`, `grep --help foo`, `python --help foo` (and in the VM if PowerShell aliases shadow).\n5. My opinion: since our tools have no `--help <topic>` mode, the closest imitation is **help wins**: `--help`/`-h` present → help stdout/0, extra args ignored; **unknown flags** (`--helpp`, `-v`, `--foo`) → stderr/64 + hint. That means possibly relaxing our current strict rule for `--help extra` (and maybe `--version extra`).\n   - Tradeoff: current strict is internally simple and locks a crisp contract, but diverges from GNU/cl.\n   - If we change: `handle_help_flag` shouldn't require argc==2; it should trigger when argv[1] is `--help`/`-h` (ignore the rest). Same for version.\n6. Whether to test more utilities: yes, a few GNU ones, to settle \"help wins vs error\". Suggest list.\n\nAlso note: the user's uncertainty \"мб при ошибке в самом help флаге stderr а при --help smth_strange - stdout?\" — I'd say: if we adopt \"help wins\", then `--help smth` → stdout/0 (help), NOT error. If we keep strict, both are errors → stderr. Mixing (help-flag-error→stderr, help+junk→stdout-success) is possible but that's exactly what \"help wins\" means for the latter. So the user's instinct aligns with GNU: `--help smth` → stdout.\n\nLet me formulate clearly and recommend testing GNU coreutils, then decide. Since the mentor's directive is explicit, I should push toward empirical settling.\n\nAlso, I should be careful: I'm in plan mode; I can run read-only commands. Can I test `ls --help foo`? On this Windows box, `ls` is an alias to Get-ChildItem, not GNU ls. Could invoke `cmd /c` ... no GNU ls on Windows. Maybe git bash's ls? Not guaranteed. I'll suggest the user test on the VM (Linux) where GNU coreutils exist: `ls --help foo`, `grep --help foo`, `cp --help foo`, `python3 --help foo`, `sed --help foo`.\n\nActually, I could suggest they run these on the VM. Good.\n\nLet me also mention: the mentor's own material 291 says `--help → stdout, 0`; 284 says help via cerr. The utilities overwhelmingly do clean-help → stdout/0, which supports 291 and my position. The remaining only question is the extra-args case.\n\nLet me write the answer concisely (the user asked to remind + opinion + whether to test more).\n\nPlan-mode: just analysis, no edits.\n\nLet me write.\n\nI'll structure:\n- Да, противоречие было; моя позиция (recap 1-2 lines).\n- Что показывают твои пробы: разбор по категориям; ключевой вывод — расхождение не stdout/stderr, а «является ли `--help <arg>` определённым режимом».\n- Коррекция: git/cmake `--help <arg>` — это их режим (help п